---
// Ranked whole-invocation wall-clock: fastest to slowest median, on one
// shared logarithmic axis, optionally with a per-kernel view behind a
// CSS-only toggle.
//
// Why a log axis. The medians this freeze produced span 123 ms to 10.8 s — a
// factor of about ninety. On a linear axis every adapter below the slowest
// collapses into a sliver against the origin, which reads as "they are all
// the same" when the whole point of the tier is that they are not. The axis is
// therefore logarithmic, labelled as such, with 1–3–10 decade ticks whose
// labels are the plain durations they stand for, and every median is also
// printed as a number beside its own row so that no reading of the chart
// depends on decoding the scale.
//
// What this adds to the contract, which is nothing. The marks are the
// aggregation `docs/latency-tier.md` preregisters — median, quartiles, and
// (as whiskers) the tenth and ninetieth percentiles of the same per-case
// wall-clock. There is no new statistic, no mean, no pooling with any
// correctness figure, and no composite. Ordering rows by median is a reading
// aid for one descriptive quantity, not a score.
//
// The rules the drawing obeys, each of them the contract's:
//
//   * phases are drawn only for the adapters whose declared row has them, are
//     indented under their own adapter, and are never set beside another
//     adapter's total — phases compare within an adapter, totals across them;
//   * an adapter that never invoked on a kernel is *absent* from that kernel's
//     view, never a zero-length bar, because declining a case is not a fast
//     answer to it;
//   * every row states how many invocations are behind it, and in a kernel
//     view how many of that kernel's assertions those were, so a median over
//     a partly-declined population cannot be misread as a median over all of
//     it;
//   * the conditions the numbers were produced under travel with the chart
//     wherever it renders, rather than living only on the page it links to.
import { latencyModel, latencyRanking, formatMs } from '../data/latency';
import { warmLatency } from '../data/warm-latency';
import {
  invocationOverhead,
  overheadMarkFor,
  SIGNIFICANCE_SHARE,
} from '../data/invocation-overhead';
import { vendorColorClass, vendorName } from '../data/snapshots';

interface Props {
  /**
   * Render the thirteen per-kernel panels behind the toggle. The landing page
   * takes the general view alone: fourteen panels of inline SVG is a fair
   * weight for the page dedicated to this tier and a poor one for a summary
   * page that already carries the evolution chart.
   */
  kernelViews?: boolean;
  /**
   * Restate the environment stamp and the run-conditions caveat inline. The
   * tier page already carries both in full above the chart; the landing page
   * does not, and the chart may not appear there without them.
   */
  stamp?: boolean;
}

const { kernelViews = true, stamp = false } = Astro.props;

const model = latencyModel();
const ranking = latencyRanking(model);
// The axis is computed across *every* view, including the kernel views this
// render may not draw, so the same duration sits at the same place on the
// landing page and on the tier page.
const panelsToRender = kernelViews ? ranking.views : ranking.views.slice(0, 1);

// ---- Geometry -------------------------------------------------------------
const width = 820;
const padLeft = 172;
const padRight = 104;
const padTop = 18;
const padBottom = 44;
const plotWidth = width - padLeft - padRight;
const mainRowHeight = 34;
const phaseRowHeight = 22;

const logMin = Math.log10(ranking.axisMin);
const logMax = Math.log10(ranking.axisMax);
const x = (value: number) => {
  const clamped = Math.min(Math.max(value, ranking.axisMin), ranking.axisMax);
  return (
    padLeft + (plotWidth * (Math.log10(clamped) - logMin)) / (logMax - logMin)
  );
};

// ---- The warm-marginal secondary mark (Amendment A15) ---------------------
//
// A row whose adapter has a *measured* warm marginal for this view's language
// carries a second, deliberately different mark: an open caret, never a bar,
// never in the ordering, and never in place of the median.
//
// Two restrictions keep the mark honest, and both are enforced here rather
// than trusted to the reader:
//
//   * it is drawn only in the kernel view whose language it was measured on.
//     The whole-corpus view mixes languages, and hanging a Java-derived
//     marginal on a mixed-language row would be a cross-population claim;
//   * a row without a measurement gets no mark at all. The amendment's rule is
//     that a decline is recorded as a decline — an absent mark reads as
//     "not measured", where a mark at zero would read as "free".
//
// The mark never moves a row: ordering stays by cold median, because the cold
// per-invocation figure remains the headline and the warm marginal is a
// separate quantity set beside it, not a correction to it.
const warmMeasurements = warmLatency().measurements;
function warmMarkFor(
  viewId: string,
  tool: string,
): [number, number] | null {
  if (viewId === 'all') return null;
  const measurement = warmMeasurements.find(
    (candidate) => candidate.tool === tool && candidate.language === viewId,
  );
  return measurement ? measurement.leastSquaresRangeMs : null;
}

// ---- The estimated-overhead secondary mark (Amendment A24) ----------------
//
// A second, differently drawn annotation: the wall-clock of one complete
// adapter invocation over a trivial no-flow fixture, which is fixed overhead
// plus near-zero analysis and therefore an *upper bound* on start-up and
// warm-up. It is an estimate, drawn as a hollow dashed *span* — never a point,
// never a solid caret — so that it can be read neither as a precise figure nor
// as A15's measured warm marginal, and it obeys the same three restrictions
// that caret does: never in the ordering, never subtracted from a median,
// absent where there is no figure.
//
// The span is the whole of it. The published figure is the range the repeats
// spanned, its width is the measurement's precision, and drawing that width is
// the only honest way to put it on a chart: a point would claim a precision
// the repeats did not have.
//
// It carries one restriction of its own. A24 draws a mark only where the
// estimate is a *substantial* share of the row it annotates — a mark at 16 ms
// on a 123 ms row is unreadable at chart scale and invites the reading that a
// mark means "slow start-up" when it would mean "a mark was drawn". The
// threshold is preregistered, relative to each row's own cold median, and
// applied to the range's *low* end so no mark appears on the strength of one
// slow repeat. Every value, marked or not, is in the latency page's table.
const overhead = invocationOverhead();
const overheadPublished = overhead.estimates;

interface Row {
  kind: 'adapter' | 'phase';
  tool: string;
  label: string;
  sublabel: string;
  d: {
    p10: number;
    q1: number;
    median: number;
    q3: number;
    p90: number;
    n: number;
  };
  /** Warm marginal per case, low to high across the retained repeats. */
  warm: [number, number] | null;
  /** Estimated per-invocation overhead range, when one clears the threshold. */
  estimate: { lowMs: number; highMs: number; language: string } | null;
  title: string;
  y: number;
}

/** One view's rows, laid out top to bottom, and the height they need. */
function layout(view: (typeof ranking.views)[number]) {
  const rows: Row[] = [];
  let y = padTop;
  for (const entry of view.entries) {
    const name = vendorName(entry.tool);
    const warm = warmMarkFor(view.id, entry.tool);
    const estimate = overheadMarkFor(view.id, entry.tool);
    y += mainRowHeight / 2;
    rows.push({
      kind: 'adapter',
      tool: entry.tool,
      label: name,
      sublabel:
        entry.covered === null
          ? `${entry.timed} timed`
          : `${entry.timed} of ${entry.covered} timed`,
      d: entry.whole,
      warm,
      estimate,
      title:
        `${name} — median ${formatMs(entry.whole.median)}, ` +
        `IQR ${formatMs(entry.whole.q1)} to ${formatMs(entry.whole.q3)}, ` +
        `p10–p90 ${formatMs(entry.whole.p10)} to ${formatMs(entry.whole.p90)}, ` +
        `over ${entry.timed} timed whole invocations` +
        (warm === null
          ? ''
          : `. Warm marginal, measured separately: ${
              warm[0] === warm[1]
                ? formatMs(warm[0])
                : `${formatMs(warm[0])} to ${formatMs(warm[1])}`
            } per case in one process, over ${
              warmMeasurements.find(
                (candidate) =>
                  candidate.tool === entry.tool && candidate.language === view.id,
              )!.runs.length
            } retained repeats`) +
        (estimate === null
          ? ''
          : `. Estimated per-invocation overhead, an upper bound from a trivial ` +
            `no-flow ${estimate.language} fixture: ${formatMs(estimate.lowMs)} to ` +
            `${formatMs(estimate.highMs)} across repeats`),
      y,
    });
    y += mainRowHeight / 2;
    for (const phase of entry.phases) {
      y += phaseRowHeight / 2;
      rows.push({
        kind: 'phase',
        tool: entry.tool,
        label: phase.phase,
        sublabel: '',
        d: phase,
        warm: null,
        estimate: null,
        title:
          `${name} phase ${phase.phase} — median ${formatMs(phase.median)}, ` +
          `IQR ${formatMs(phase.q1)} to ${formatMs(phase.q3)}, ` +
          `p10–p90 ${formatMs(phase.p10)} to ${formatMs(phase.p90)}. ` +
          `Comparable only against this adapter's own numbers.`,
        y,
      });
      y += phaseRowHeight / 2;
    }
  }
  return { rows, height: y + padBottom };
}

// A suffix keeps element identifiers unique when both renders of this
// component end up in one document, and keeps the radio group of one render
// from capturing the labels of another.
const scope = kernelViews ? 'k' : 'g';
const panels = panelsToRender.map((view) => ({
  view,
  ...layout(view),
  radioId: `lat-${scope}-view-${view.id}`,
  panelId: `lat-${scope}-panel-${view.id}`,
}));

// The toggle is a plain radio group, exactly as the cross-snapshot evolution
// chart's is: no JavaScript, keyboard-operable by default, and it degrades to
// a list of labelled radios if the stylesheet never arrives. Fourteen options
// need fourteen `:checked ~ …` pairs, and hand-writing them would be a list
// that silently rots the next time a kernel is added — so they are emitted
// from the same array the panels are, and cannot drift from it.
//
// The selected pill takes the accent rather than the evolution toggle's
// `--sl-color-gray-6`: with two options a subtle fill is enough to find, and
// in the dark theme gray-6 happens to equal the ribbon's own background, so
// the only cue left is the label's colour. With fourteen options that is not
// enough, and the accent reads against the ribbon in both themes.
const toggleCss = panels
  .map(
    (panel) => `
#${panel.radioId}:checked ~ .lat-ribbon label[for='${panel.radioId}'] {
  background: var(--sl-color-accent-low);
  color: var(--sl-color-white);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--sl-color-accent);
}
#${panel.radioId}:focus-visible ~ .lat-ribbon label[for='${panel.radioId}'] {
  outline: 2px solid var(--sl-color-accent-high);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
#${panel.radioId}:checked ~ .lat-panels > #${panel.panelId} {
  display: block;
}`,
  )
  .join('\n');

const general = ranking.views[0]!;
const fastest = general.entries[0]!;
const slowest = general.entries.at(-1)!;
const decomposedCount = general.entries.filter(
  (entry) => entry.phases.length > 0,
).length;
const environment = model.environments[0];
// Whether this render actually draws a caret: the marks live in the per-kernel
// views, so the whole-corpus-only render (the landing page's) carries the
// annotation in prose and no key for a symbol it never draws.
const drawsWarmMarks =
  kernelViews &&
  panels.some((panel) =>
    panel.rows.some((row) => row.warm !== null),
  );
// The estimate marks, unlike the warm caret, do appear in the whole-corpus
// view: an adapter's invocation overhead is a property of its invocation
// shape, measured on one named language, and that language is stated on the
// row and in the caption rather than being read as language-free.
const drawsEstimateMarks = panels.some((panel) =>
  panel.rows.some((row) => row.estimate !== null),
);
// Adapters whose estimate was published but sits below the threshold: named
// in the caption so an absent mark reads as "small", never as "unmeasured".
const belowThreshold = overheadPublished.filter(
  (estimate) => !estimate.significant,
);
---

<div class="latency-ranking">
  {kernelViews &&
    panels.map((panel, index) => (
      <input
        class="lat-radio"
        type="radio"
        name={`latency-ranking-${scope}`}
        id={panel.radioId}
        checked={index === 0}
      />
    ))}

  {kernelViews && (
    <div class="lat-ribbon" role="group" aria-label="Latency ranking view">
      {panels.map((panel) => (
        <label for={panel.radioId}>{panel.view.label}</label>
      ))}
    </div>
  )}

  <div class="lat-panels">
    {panels.map((panel) => (
      <section
        class:list={['lat-panel', !kernelViews && 'lat-panel-only']}
        id={panel.panelId}
      >
        <div class="chart-frame">
          <svg
            class="ranking-chart"
            viewBox={`0 0 ${width} ${panel.height}`}
            role="img"
            aria-labelledby={`${panel.panelId}-title ${panel.panelId}-desc`}
          >
            <title id={`${panel.panelId}-title`}>
              {panel.view.population === null
                ? 'Whole-invocation wall-clock per analyzer, over every timed invocation in the freeze'
                : `Whole-invocation wall-clock per analyzer on the ${panel.view.label} kernel`}
            </title>
            <desc id={`${panel.panelId}-desc`}>
              One row per analyzer, ordered fastest median first, on a
              logarithmic time axis. Each row draws the tenth to ninetieth
              percentile as a thin line, the interquartile range as a bar, and
              the median as a thick tick, with the median also printed as a
              number. Indented rows are the declared phases of the two adapters
              whose invocation exposes more than one subprocess, and belong to
              the adapter above them only. The latency page carries every
              value, including the minima and maxima this chart does not draw,
              in the data tables behind its "Show the data table" disclosures.
              A solid caret below a row is that adapter's separately measured
              warm marginal per case; a dashed span above a row is its
              estimated per-invocation overhead, an upper bound measured on a
              trivial no-flow fixture, drawn across the range its repeats
              spanned rather than at a point. Neither is a cold median, neither
              affects the ordering, and neither is subtracted from anything.
            </desc>

            {ranking.ticks.map((tick) => (
              <>
                <line
                  class="grid"
                  x1={x(tick.value)}
                  x2={x(tick.value)}
                  y1={padTop - 6}
                  y2={panel.height - padBottom + 6}
                />
                <text
                  class="tick"
                  x={x(tick.value)}
                  y={panel.height - padBottom + 22}
                  text-anchor="middle"
                >
                  {tick.label}
                </text>
              </>
            ))}

            <line
              class="axis"
              x1={padLeft}
              x2={padLeft + plotWidth}
              y1={panel.height - padBottom + 6}
              y2={panel.height - padBottom + 6}
            />
            <text
              class="axis-title"
              x={padLeft + plotWidth / 2}
              y={panel.height - padBottom + 39}
              text-anchor="middle"
            >
              wall-clock per invocation — logarithmic scale
            </text>

            {panel.rows.map((row) => {
              const boxHeight = row.kind === 'adapter' ? 13 : 7;
              const medianHeight = row.kind === 'adapter' ? 21 : 13;
              return (
                <g
                  class:list={[
                    'row',
                    row.kind === 'phase' && 'phase-row',
                    vendorColorClass(row.tool),
                  ]}
                >
                  <title>{row.title}</title>
                  <text
                    class={row.kind === 'adapter' ? 'row-label' : 'phase-label'}
                    x={padLeft - 12}
                    y={row.kind === 'adapter' ? row.y - 1 : row.y + 4}
                    text-anchor="end"
                  >
                    {row.label}
                  </text>
                  {row.sublabel !== '' && (
                    <text
                      class="row-sublabel"
                      x={padLeft - 12}
                      y={row.y + 12}
                      text-anchor="end"
                    >
                      {row.sublabel}
                    </text>
                  )}
                  <line
                    class="whisker"
                    x1={x(row.d.p10)}
                    x2={x(row.d.p90)}
                    y1={row.y}
                    y2={row.y}
                  />
                  <line
                    class="whisker-cap"
                    x1={x(row.d.p10)}
                    x2={x(row.d.p10)}
                    y1={row.y - boxHeight / 2}
                    y2={row.y + boxHeight / 2}
                  />
                  <line
                    class="whisker-cap"
                    x1={x(row.d.p90)}
                    x2={x(row.d.p90)}
                    y1={row.y - boxHeight / 2}
                    y2={row.y + boxHeight / 2}
                  />
                  <rect
                    class="box"
                    x={x(row.d.q1)}
                    y={row.y - boxHeight / 2}
                    width={Math.max(1.5, x(row.d.q3) - x(row.d.q1))}
                    height={boxHeight}
                    rx="2"
                  />
                  <line
                    class="median"
                    x1={x(row.d.median)}
                    x2={x(row.d.median)}
                    y1={row.y - medianHeight / 2}
                    y2={row.y + medianHeight / 2}
                  />
                  {row.warm !== null && (
                    <>
                      <line
                        class="warm-span"
                        x1={x(row.warm[0])}
                        x2={x(row.warm[1])}
                        y1={row.y + 11}
                        y2={row.y + 11}
                      />
                      {[row.warm[0], row.warm[1]].map((value) => (
                        <polyline
                          class="warm-mark"
                          points={`${x(value) - 4},${row.y + 14} ${x(value)},${row.y + 8} ${x(value) + 4},${row.y + 14}`}
                          fill="none"
                        />
                      ))}
                    </>
                  )}
                  {row.estimate !== null &&
                    (() => {
                      // The span is the figure. A range whose repeats agreed
                      // closely would otherwise collapse to a hairline on a
                      // log axis and read as a point claim, so it is drawn at
                      // a floor width — the end caps still mark where the
                      // range actually falls.
                      const left = x(row.estimate.lowMs);
                      const right = Math.max(x(row.estimate.highMs), left + 6);
                      const top = row.y - 13;
                      return (
                        <>
                          <line
                            class="estimate-span"
                            x1={left}
                            x2={right}
                            y1={top}
                            y2={top}
                          />
                          <line
                            class="estimate-cap"
                            x1={left}
                            x2={left}
                            y1={top - 4}
                            y2={top + 4}
                          />
                          <line
                            class="estimate-cap"
                            x1={right}
                            x2={right}
                            y1={top - 4}
                            y2={top + 4}
                          />
                        </>
                      );
                    })()}
                  <text
                    class={row.kind === 'adapter' ? 'row-value' : 'phase-value'}
                    x={padLeft + plotWidth + 12}
                    y={row.y + 4}
                    text-anchor="start"
                  >
                    {formatMs(row.d.median)}
                  </text>
                </g>
              );
            })}
          </svg>
        </div>

        <p class="caption">
          {panel.view.population === null ? (
            <>
              Every timed analyzer invocation the freeze binds —{' '}
              {model.totalTimed} of them, across every score tier and both
              model profiles. This is the widest denominator on the site and
              the only one here that is not a single population: an adapter's
              median mixes the languages it runs on, whose fixtures differ in
              size and whose front ends differ in cost.{' '}
              {kernelViews ? (
                <>The per-kernel views hold the language fixed.</>
              ) : (
                <>
                  The{' '}
                  <a href="/snapshots/v0-6-0/latency/#ranking">
                    per-kernel views on the latency page
                  </a>{' '}
                  hold the language fixed.
                </>
              )}
            </>
          ) : (
            <>
              The <code>{panel.view.label}</code> kernel's{' '}
              {panel.view.population} benchmark-controlled <code>core</code>{' '}
              assertions, the same no-pooling population the correctness
              sections read. {panel.view.entries.length} of the{' '}
              {general.entries.length} analyzers invoked here at all; the rest
              do not cover this kernel and are{' '}
              <strong>absent from this view rather than drawn at zero</strong>.
              Where a row's count is below {panel.view.population}, the
              remaining assertions were declined before any subprocess was
              spawned and so have nothing to time.
            </>
          )}
        </p>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>

  <ul class="chart-legend">
    <li class="key-median">
      <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      median, printed beside every row
    </li>
    <li class="key-box">
      <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      interquartile range (Q1–Q3)
    </li>
    <li class="key-whisker">
      <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      p10–p90; the minimum and maximum are in the tables, not the whiskers
    </li>
    <li class="key-phase">
      <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      indented rows: an adapter's own declared phases
    </li>
    {drawsWarmMarks && (
      <li class="key-warm">
        <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <strong>measured</strong>&nbsp;warm marginal per case — the range its
        retained repeats span
      </li>
    )}
    {drawsEstimateMarks && (
      <li class="key-estimate">
        <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <strong>estimated</strong>&nbsp;per-invocation overhead (trivial
        fixture, upper bound), drawn across the range its repeats spanned, where
        that range starts at or above{' '}
        {Math.round(SIGNIFICANCE_SHARE * 100)}% of the row's median
      </li>
    )}
  </ul>
</div>

{warmMeasurements.length > 0 && (
  <p class="caption">
    <strong>Every bar above is cold per-invocation wall-clock, and the warm
    marginal is a different quantity measured separately.</strong> Cold is what
    this benchmark actually runs — one process per case, start-up inside the
    number, because start-up is not observable from inside a single invocation.
    Read across runtimes, though, those bars overstate the steady-state gap: a
    JVM engine's row carries a JVM start a long-lived deployment pays once. So
    the other quantity is measured directly rather than estimated and
    subtracted — <em>k</em> cases through one tool process, for increasing
    <em>k</em>, reporting the <em>slope</em> of batch wall-clock against{' '}
    <em>k</em>.
    {drawsWarmMarks ? (
      <>
        {' '}The carets mark it on the rows that have one, and they mark a{' '}
        <em>range</em>: the whole series is measured more than once and the
        figure published is the span its repeats cover, so the mark's width is
        its own precision rather than a number stated more exactly than it was
        measured. It is never subtracted from a median, never substituted for
        one, and never used to order the rows.
      </>
    ) : (
      <>
        {' '}No caret is drawn in this view: the whole-corpus view mixes
        languages, and a marginal measured on one kernel is not a claim about a
        mixed-language row. The per-kernel views carry the marks.
      </>
    )}{' '}
    Only adapters whose released CLI exposes a real multi-case batch have a
    figure at all; the rest are{' '}
    <strong>not observable with the released CLI</strong>, and the{' '}
    <a href="/snapshots/v0-6-0/latency/#warm-marginal">
      warm-marginal section
    </a>{' '}
    records every verdict, measured and declined, with the evidence behind it.
  </p>
)}

{overheadPublished.length > 0 && (
  <p class="caption">
    <strong>The dashed spans are estimates, not measurements of the same kind
    as the bars.</strong> Each is the wall-clock of one complete
    adapter invocation — same pipeline, same policy, rule or query, same
    subprocess shape — over a <em>trivial no-flow fixture</em>: both benchmark
    endpoints declared, nothing connecting them, nothing to find. That is fixed
    per-invocation overhead <em>plus</em> the trivial fixture's own near-zero
    analysis, so it is an <strong>upper bound</strong> on what an adapter pays
    before it starts work, and it is a cold single-shot execution — the same
    posture the bars are measured in, and not a steady-state one. It is never
    subtracted from a median, never substituted for one, and never used to
    order the rows.{' '}
    <strong>The width of a span is the figure's precision, not a
    decoration:</strong> each measurement is repeated a fixed number of times
    and what is published is the range those repeats spanned — never a mean,
    never one chosen repeat, and never withheld for repeats that disagree,
    because a disagreement widens the range and that is the honest consequence
    of it.{' '}
    {drawsEstimateMarks && (
      <>
        A span is drawn only where the range <em>starts</em> at or above{' '}
        {Math.round(SIGNIFICANCE_SHARE * 100)}% of that row's own cold median —
        a threshold fixed in the amendment before any estimate was measured,
        read at the low end so that no mark can appear on the strength of one
        slow repeat.{' '}
        {belowThreshold.length > 0 && (
          <>
            The{' '}
            {belowThreshold
              .map((estimate) => vendorName(estimate.tool))
              .join(', ')}{' '}
            {belowThreshold.length === 1 ? 'estimate is' : 'estimates are'}{' '}
            below it and{' '}
            {belowThreshold.length === 1 ? 'carries' : 'carry'} no mark —{' '}
            <strong>small, not unmeasured</strong>.{' '}
          </>
        )}
      </>
    )}
    Each estimate is measured in one named language, stated on the row it
    annotates, because boot cost is not language-free.{' '}
    <a href="/dataflowbench/snapshots/v0-6-0/latency/#invocation-overhead">
      The estimates table
    </a>{' '}
    carries every value — every repeat behind every range, every unmarked row,
    and every adapter for which no estimate could be taken at all.
  </p>
)}

<p class="caption">
  <strong>The axis is logarithmic.</strong> Each labelled tick is three times
  the one before it, so equal distances are equal <em>ratios</em>, not equal
  durations — the gap from 100&nbsp;ms to 300&nbsp;ms is drawn the same width
  as the gap from 10&nbsp;s to 30&nbsp;s. That is the only way this freeze's
  medians fit in one picture: they span {formatMs(fastest.whole.median)} to{' '}
  {formatMs(slowest.whole.median)}, and on a linear axis every analyzer except
  the slowest would be a sliver against the origin. Because a log axis is easy
  to misread, every median is also printed as a number at the right of its own
  row.
</p>

<p class="caption">
  <strong>The indented rows are phases, and they are not comparable across
  adapters.</strong> Only the {decomposedCount} adapters whose preregistered
  row declares more than one subprocess have them. A phase mark sits on the
  same axis as the totals because it is the same kind of measurement —
  wall-clock of a subprocess — but reading one adapter's phase against another
  adapter's total is precisely the comparison the granularity rule forbids.
  Read a phase against the adapter it is indented under, and nothing else.
</p>

<p class="caption">
  <strong>Ordering is not scoring, and this is never pooled with
  correctness.</strong> Rows are sorted by median because an unsorted ranking
  is unreadable, not because latency is a result. No correctness figure appears
  in this chart and no number here is blended with one: there is no combined
  score anywhere on this site, and a fast analyzer that answers wrongly is
  neither rewarded nor penalised by anything drawn above. Cases an analyzer
  declined before invocation are <strong>absent, not entered as zero</strong> —
  entering them as zero would make the analyzers that decline the most look the
  fastest, which is exactly backwards.
</p>

{stamp && (
  <p class="caption">
    <strong>The conditions these numbers were produced under.</strong> A single
    developer machine{' '}
    {environment && (
      <>
        (<code>{environment.hardwareModel}</code>, <code>{environment.os}</code>{' '}
        <code>{environment.osRelease}</code>, {environment.cpuCount} logical
        CPUs),
      </>
    )} under light concurrent load, running the benchmark's standing
    sequential-run discipline — one analyzer at a time, never two at once.
    <strong>
      Latency numbers are comparable within one environment and are not
      comparable across machines.
    </strong>{' '}
    The bars carry no repeated trials and no warm-up iterations: the case
    population is the sample and its spread is the statistics, so the whiskers
    are that spread and not error bars on a repeated measurement.{' '}
    <strong>
      Every number is a cold start: each case spawns a fresh process, so JVM
      tools (Joern, OpenTaint, FlowDroid) pay full JVM start-up in every
      invocation, and Pysa pays Pyre initialization.
    </strong>{' '}
    In a resident deployment those costs amortize to once per session, so
    cross-runtime comparisons here overstate the steady-state gap; the boot
    share of a single invocation is not adapter-observable and is included,
    stated rather than estimated.{' '}
    {warmMeasurements.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <strong>How much they overstate it is now measured, not left as a
        caveat</strong> — separately, as the{' '}
        <a href="/snapshots/v0-6-0/latency/#warm-marginal">
          warm marginal cost
        </a>{' '}
        of one more case in a process that has already started, wherever the
        released CLI let it be measured.{' '}
      </>
    )}
    {overheadPublished.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <strong>And what an invocation costs before it has anything to find is
        estimated for every adapter it could be estimated for</strong> — the{' '}
        <a href="/dataflowbench/snapshots/v0-6-0/latency/#invocation-overhead">
          per-invocation overhead
        </a>{' '}
        of one complete invocation over a trivial no-flow fixture, an upper
        bound published across the range its repeats spanned, never subtracted
        from a bar above.{' '}
      </>
    )}
    Read this as characterization of what the
    benchmark actually costs to run, at the order-of-magnitude and shape
    level, and not as a precise figure for any engine. The full contract, the per-adapter granularity, the cache
    declarations and every quartile are on the{' '}
    <a href="/snapshots/v0-6-0/latency/">latency page</a>.
  </p>
)}

